 177: 
 178: \section{Real-World Validation on KITTI Dataset}
 179: \label{sec:kitti}
 180: 
 181: \subsection{Dataset and Ground Truth}
 182: To evaluate the ARF-Net beyond pristine simulation, we transitioned the framework to the KITTI Vision Benchmark \cite{geiger}. We processed all 7,481 training frames, extracting 5,897 VRU detections (Pedestrian and Cyclist classes). To isolate the performance of the risk-assessment module---rather than conflating it with the failure modes of a specific object detector---we parse KITTI's ground-truth \texttt{label\_2} annotations and intentionally inject synthetic Gaussian noise ($\sigma = 0.3$m, applied to 20\% of samples) to simulate realistic bounding box jitter.
 183: 
 184: Since KITTI provides single frames rather than tracked sequences, true per-frame velocities are unavailable. We therefore estimate VRU velocities by sampling from class-typical kinematic priors: $v \sim U(0.5, 2.0)$ m/s for pedestrians and $v \sim U(2.0, 8.0)$ m/s for cyclists, oriented along the annotated \texttt{rotation\_y} heading. To strictly prevent data leakage from nearly identical sequential driving scenes, we employ a frame-ID-based 70-30 train-test split rather than randomly splitting the bounding boxes.
 185: 
 186: We define a composite ground truth label based on physical risk geometry rather than a single distance threshold. A VRU is labeled as ``high-risk'' if it satisfies three simultaneous conditions: (1) Euclidean distance $< 15$m from the AV, (2) lateral offset $|x| < 3$m from the AV's forward axis (i.e., the VRU is in or near the driving lane), and (3) the VRU is in front of the AV ($z > 0$). This criterion is designed to be independent of both models while requiring directional awareness to predict---a VRU at 10m on the sidewalk ($|x| = 8$m) is safe, while a VRU at 10m in the lane ($|x| = 1$m) is dangerous.
 187: 
 188: \subsection{Baseline Comparisons}
 189: We evaluate three approaches:
 190: \begin{itemize}
 191:     \item \textbf{Rule-Based TTC ($< 3.0$s)}: The standard Time-to-Collision metric, computed using the radial closing speed between the AV and VRU.
 192:     \item \textbf{ML Baseline}: A Gradient Boosting Classifier trained on raw kinematics (distance, AV velocity, VRU velocity, relative velocity).
 193:     \item \textbf{Proposed ARF-Net}: The same classifier augmented with the Hessian eigenvalues ($\lambda_1, \lambda_2$) and determinant.
 194: \end{itemize}
 195: 
 196: \subsection{Results}
 197: 
 198: \begin{table}[htbp]
 199: \centering
 200: \caption{Performance on the KITTI Vision Benchmark}
 201: \label{tab:kitti_results}
 202: \begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
 203: \toprule
 204: \textbf{Metric} & \textbf{TTC ($<3.0$s)} & \textbf{ML Baseline} & \textbf{ARF-Net} \\ \midrule
 205: Average Precision (AP) & 0.238 & 0.650 & \textbf{0.747} \\
 206: False Positives & 370 & 118 & \textbf{93} \\
 207: False Negatives & --- & 118 & \textbf{96} \\ \bottomrule
 208: \end{tabular}
 209: \end{table}
 210: 
 211: The rule-based TTC threshold failed catastrophically (AP = 0.238, 370 false positives), confirming that isotropic distance-over-speed metrics are fundamentally inadequate for the directional ground truth. The ML Baseline improved substantially (AP = 0.650) by learning non-linear decision boundaries, but still generated 118 phantom braking events.
 212: 
 213: The ARF-Net achieved the highest AP (0.747), representing a \textbf{9.7\% absolute improvement} over the ML Baseline. More critically, it reduced false positives from 118 to 93---a \textbf{21.2\% reduction in phantom braking}---while simultaneously reducing false negatives from 118 to 96 (18.6\% fewer missed detections). Feature importance analysis confirmed that the Hessian eigenvalue $\lambda_1$ was the second most important feature (12.2\%), validating that the ARF's geometric decomposition provides information that raw kinematics alone cannot capture.
 214: 
 215: \subsection{Limitations}
 216: \label{sec:limitations}
 217: We acknowledge several limitations of the current evaluation. First, VRU velocities on KITTI were estimated from class-typical kinematic priors rather than measured from tracked sequences; future work will apply this framework to multi-frame datasets (e.g., Waymo Open) where per-frame velocity estimation is possible. Second, the injected Gaussian noise is a simplified approximation of real perception error; true sensor failures (e.g., LiDAR point cloud sparsity, severe occlusion) produce systematic, non-Gaussian artifacts. Future iterations will integrate the ARF module directly with the output heads of 3D object detectors such as PointPillars or CenterPoint \cite{lang} to evaluate robustness under authentic perception noise. Third, while the Gradient Boosting Classifier serves as a transparent proof-of-concept, exploring lightweight neural architectures may further improve performance.
 218: 
 219: \section{Conclusion and Future Work}
 220: \label{sec:conclusion}
 221: This research successfully conceptualized, implemented, and validated an explainable safety framework for Autonomous Vehicles encountering Vulnerable Road Users. By moving beyond rudimentary isotropic models like Time-to-Collision, we developed an Anisotropic Risk Field that models the directional intent and physical momentum of both pedestrians and cyclists. Through the extraction of Hessian eigenvalues, our system measures topological safety ruptures and translates them into human-readable telemetry via Evidence-Grounded Reasoning Traces (EGRT).
 222: 
 223: Our validation on the KITTI Vision Benchmark demonstrated that the ARF features provide genuine, measurable improvements even under a strict, leak-free evaluation protocol: a 9.7\% AP gain and a 21.2\% reduction in phantom braking over a kinematics-only baseline. Most importantly, every collision warning produced by the system is accompanied by a deterministic, auditable reasoning trace---ensuring that safety-critical decisions are mathematically justifiable rather than opaque.
 224: 
 225: Future work will explore extending the ARF matrices into 3D space utilizing complete Point Cloud data, integrating with real-time 3D object detectors, and incorporating temporal sequence modeling (e.g., LSTMs or Transformers) to track eigenvalue ruptures over longer time horizons.
