  36: \section{Introduction}
  37: \label{sec:intro}
  38: The rapid advancement of Autonomous Driving Systems (ADS) promises a paradigm shift in urban mobility, potentially reducing traffic accidents caused by human error. However, urban environments are highly stochastic, characterized by the unpredictable behavior of Vulnerable Road Users (VRUs) such as pedestrians, cyclists, and micromobility users. Unlike vehicles constrained by lane markings and road geometry, VRUs can change direction and velocity rapidly and unpredictably. Predicting and mitigating near-miss events involving VRUs remains one of the most critical hurdles for the widespread deployment of autonomous vehicles (AVs).
  39: 
  40: Historically, the automotive industry has relied on classical Surrogate Safety Measures (SSMs) to evaluate collision risk. The most ubiquitous metric is Time-to-Collision (TTC), defined as the remaining time before two objects collide given their current relative velocity and trajectory. While computationally inexpensive and universally understood, TTC suffers from a critical limitation: it assumes isotropic risk. A pedestrian standing on a curb possesses the same radial distance metric as a pedestrian stepping into the crosswalk, despite the latter presenting a significantly higher collision risk. This inability to account for the \textit{directional intent} of the VRU often leads to false positives, colloquially known as ``phantom braking,'' which degrades passenger comfort and can induce rear-end collisions.
  41: 
  42: In recent years, the industry has pivoted towards data-driven machine learning methodologies to assess risk. While ensemble methods and neural network architectures can learn complex decision boundaries from raw sensor data, they inherently operate as ``black boxes.'' When a proprietary autonomous system initiates an emergency brake, safety engineers---and eventually, regulatory bodies---cannot easily query the system to understand the exact geometric rationale behind the decision.
  43: 
  44: To bridge the gap between simplistic isotropic metrics and opaque learned models, this paper proposes a hybrid approach: an Anisotropic Risk Field (ARF) integrated with Evidence-Grounded Reasoning Traces (EGRT). We hypothesize that by explicitly modeling the risk footprint of a VRU as an oriented ellipse---stretching longitudinally for high-momentum cyclists and laterally for pedestrians with high turning flexibility---we can generate mathematically verifiable signals of danger that complement raw kinematic features.
  45: 
  46: The primary contributions of this paper are fourfold:
  47: \begin{enumerate}
  48:     \item We formulate an Anisotropic Risk Field (ARF) relying on the Hessian eigenvalues of a 2D Gaussian distribution to measure directional topological rupture.
  49:     \item We introduce Evidence-Grounded Reasoning Traces (EGRT), a telemetry-logging mechanism that translates mathematical risk metrics into explicit, human-readable explanations.
  50:     \item We develop a rigorously defined 2D kinematic simulation engine to generate thousands of verifiable near-miss interactions, demonstrating the theoretical validity of our explainable safety framework.
  51:     \item We validate the framework on the real-world KITTI Vision Benchmark utilizing a rigorous frame-based split, demonstrating a 9.7\% AP improvement and a 21.2\% reduction in phantom braking over a kinematics-only baseline.
  52: \end{enumerate}
  53: 
  54: \section{Related Work}
  55: \label{sec:related}
  56: 
  57: \subsection{Surrogate Safety Measures}
  58: The foundation of collision avoidance in intelligent transportation relies heavily on Surrogate Safety Measures. Hayward (1972) first formalized Time-to-Collision (TTC), which has since become the gold standard for defining near-miss events \cite{hayward}. Later adaptations include Post-Encroachment Time (PET) and Time-To-React (TTR). However, researchers have repeatedly noted that TTC fails in complex urban intersections where paths are not strictly linear. According to \cite{vogel}, TTC is poorly equipped to handle the lateral movements characteristic of pedestrians, highlighting the need for two-dimensional spatial metrics.
  59: 
  60: \subsection{Artificial Potential Fields}
  61: The concept of Artificial Potential Fields (APF) was introduced by Khatib in the context of robotics to enable real-time obstacle avoidance \cite{khatib}. By assigning repulsive forces to obstacles and attractive forces to the goal, a robot can navigate smoothly. Wang et al. extended this to the ``Driving Safety Field Theory,'' modeling the risk of surrounding traffic \cite{wang}. More recently, Zhang et al. proposed ``Anisotropic Safety Potential Fields,'' recognizing that vehicles and bicycles have directional momentum \cite{zhang}. Our work extends this lineage by specifically tuning the anisotropic covariance matrices to the unique kinematic profiles of distinct VRU classes (pedestrians vs. cyclists) and extracting the Hessian matrix for rapid safety thresholding.
  62: 
  63: \subsection{Explainable AI (XAI) in Autonomous Vehicles}
  64: As regulatory scrutiny over autonomous vehicles increases, Explainable AI (XAI) has become an urgent research priority. Techniques like SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) and Grad-CAM are frequently used to interpret neural networks \cite{lundberg}. However, these post-hoc explanation methods are computationally heavy and often visual, rather than semantic. For a safety-critical system, engineers require instantaneous, semantic telemetry. Our proposed EGRT framework is inspired by rule-based expert systems, providing deterministic, semantic explanations immediately alongside the prediction, circumventing the need for computationally expensive post-hoc analysis.
  65: 
  66: \section{Methodology}
  67: \label{sec:method}
  68: 
  69: \subsection{System Architecture}
  70: Our framework consists of three primary modules: the Kinematic Simulator, the ARF Feature Extractor, and the EGRT Generator. The simulator produces state-space representations of AV-VRU interactions. The ARF Extractor processes these coordinates to compute the Hessian matrix of the risk field. Finally, a Gradient Boosting Classifier predicts near-miss events, and the EGRT Generator translates the underlying geometric features into human-readable telemetry logs.
  71: 
  72: \subsection{2D Kinematic Simulation Engine}
  73: Generating a statistically significant dataset of dangerous near-misses in the real world is both difficult and ethically precarious. While large-scale datasets like nuScenes and Waymo Open Dataset exist, extracting thousands of perfectly annotated near-misses is computationally prohibitive. Thus, we engineered a custom 2D kinematic simulator in Python.
  74: 
  75: We define the state of the Autonomous Vehicle (AV) as $S_{AV} = [x_a, y_a, v_a, \theta_a]$, where $(x_a, y_a)$ is the 2D Cartesian position, $v_a$ is the scalar velocity, and $\theta_a$ is the heading angle. Similarly, the VRU state is defined as $S_{VRU} = [x_v, y_v, v_v, \theta_v]$.
  76: 
  77: The simulation operates with a discrete time step $\Delta t = 0.1$ seconds. The position updates follow standard kinematic equations of motion:
  78: \begin{align}
  79:     x(t+1) &= x(t) + v \cdot \cos(\theta) \cdot \Delta t + \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_{noise}) \\
  80:     y(t+1) &= y(t) + v \cdot \sin(\theta) \cdot \Delta t + \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_{noise})
  81: \end{align}
  82: To simulate the unpredictable nature of human walking, we inject Gaussian noise $\mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_{noise})$ into the VRU trajectory, representing slight lateral drifts and micro-adjustments during crossing. The simulator forces paths to intersect, recording the precise frame of minimum Euclidean distance between the entities.
  83: 
  84: \subsection{Anisotropic Risk Field Formulation}
  85: We model the danger zone surrounding a VRU as a dynamically stretched Gaussian distribution. Let $\mathbf{p} = [x_v - x_a, y_v - y_a]^T$ be the relative position vector of the VRU with respect to the AV.
  86: 
  87: We define a diagonal variance matrix $\bm{\Lambda}$ representing the base uncertainty. For pedestrians, lateral uncertainty $\sigma_{lat}$ is heavily weighted, as they can step off a curb instantaneously. For cyclists, longitudinal uncertainty $\sigma_{long}$ dominates due to their forward momentum. Both are modeled as velocity-dependent functions. The specific scalar multipliers (e.g., $\sigma_{lat} = 2.0 + 0.5 v_v$ for pedestrians) were empirically tuned based on average human walking kinematics (1.2--2.0 m/s) and cycling kinematics (4.0--8.0 m/s) from established biomechanics literature:
  88: \begin{equation}
  89:     \bm{\Lambda} = 
  90:     \begin{bmatrix}
  91:     \sigma_{long}^2(v_v) & 0 \\
  92:     0 & \sigma_{lat}^2(v_v)
  93:     \end{bmatrix}
  94: \end{equation}
  95: 
  96: To align the risk field with the VRU's direction of travel, we apply a 2D rotation matrix $\mathbf{R}(\theta_v)$:
  97: \begin{equation}
  98:     \mathbf{R}(\theta_v) = 
  99:     \begin{bmatrix}
 100:     \cos(\theta_v) & -\sin(\theta_v) \\
 101:     \sin(\theta_v) & \cos(\theta_v)
 102:     \end{bmatrix}
 103: \end{equation}
 104: 
 105: The final Anisotropic Covariance Matrix $\bm{\Sigma}$ is computed as:
 106: \begin{equation}
 107:     \bm{\Sigma} = \mathbf{R} \bm{\Lambda} \mathbf{R}^T
 108: \end{equation}
 109: 
 110: The risk potential value $U(\mathbf{p})$ experienced by the AV at its relative distance $\mathbf{p}$ is given by the multivariate Gaussian density function (ignoring the normalization constant for purely relative scaling):
 111: \begin{equation}
 112:     U(\mathbf{p}) = \exp \left( -\frac{1}{2} \mathbf{p}^T \bm{\Sigma}^{-1} \mathbf{p} \right)
 113: \end{equation}
 114: 
 115: \subsection{Hessian Analysis and Topological Rupture}
 116: While the risk potential $U(\mathbf{p})$ provides a scalar value of danger, it lacks geometric context. To understand \textit{how} the risk field is changing---i.e., whether the AV is piercing the side of the field (lateral crossing) or approaching it head-on---we compute the Hessian matrix $\mathbf{H}$ of the risk potential:
 117: \begin{equation}
 118:     \mathbf{H} = U(\mathbf{p}) \cdot \left( (\bm{\Sigma}^{-1}\mathbf{p})(\bm{\Sigma}^{-1}\mathbf{p})^T - \bm{\Sigma}^{-1} \right)
 119: \end{equation}
 120: 
 121: We perform an eigendecomposition on $\mathbf{H}$ to extract the principal curvatures, denoted as eigenvalues $\lambda_1$ and $\lambda_2$. By sorting these such that $\lambda_2$ corresponds to the longitudinal axis and $\lambda_1$ corresponds to the lateral axis, we can interpret the geometric nature of the threat. A severe spike in these eigenvalues represents a ``Topological Rupture'' in the safety manifold. 
 122: If $\lambda_2 \gg \lambda_1$, the danger is primarily longitudinal (e.g., a cyclist braking suddenly). If $\lambda_1 \gg \lambda_2$, the danger is lateral (e.g., a pedestrian stepping out from between parked cars).
 123: 
 124: \subsection{Evidence-Grounded Reasoning Traces (EGRT)}
 125: To achieve full explainability, we designed an EGRT generator that maps the computed eigenvalues ($\lambda_1, \lambda_2$) and the Euclidean distance to deterministic, human-readable strings. Unlike black-box models that merely output a binary brake command, our system logs telemetry in the following format:
 126: 
 127: \begin{quote}
 128: \texttt{[EGRT] TRG:RUPTURE | L2:0.10 > L1:0.06 | DIST:1.2m | DYN:LONGITUDINAL | ACTION:BRAKE}
 129: \end{quote}
 130: 
 131: This structure ensures that any predicted near-miss can be immediately audited. Safety engineers can verify that the system correctly identified the dominant dynamic (longitudinal) and correctly assessed the geometric rupture thresholds before issuing an actuator command.
