  28: \begin{abstract}
  29: As autonomous vehicles (AVs) increasingly transition from controlled environments to complex urban landscapes, ensuring the safety of Vulnerable Road Users (VRUs)---specifically pedestrians and cyclists---has become a paramount challenge. Traditional safety surrogate measures, such as Time-to-Collision (TTC), often assume that risk is isotropically distributed, ignoring the inherent directional intent of human movement. Furthermore, the modern reliance on opaque machine learning architectures has introduced a ``black-box'' paradigm where vehicles execute emergency maneuvers without verifiable, human-readable rationale. This paper presents a novel framework integrating an Anisotropic Risk Field (ARF) model with Evidence-Grounded Reasoning Traces (EGRT) for explainable near-miss detection. By representing VRU spatial risk as a dynamically oriented 2D Gaussian distribution, we compute the Hessian matrix of the risk field and extract its eigenvalues to assess geometric ``topological ruptures.'' We first validate the theoretical framework using a custom-built 2D kinematic simulation engine across 5,000 crossing scenarios, establishing transparent, rule-based explainability. We then transition the framework to the real-world KITTI Vision Benchmark (7,481 frames, 5,897 VRU detections), where we utilize a frame-based split to mitigate data leakage and define a composite ground truth based on proximity and lateral lane occupancy. While the simulation proves our framework's interpretability, the KITTI validation demonstrates its performance robustness. Our empirical results show that the ARF-Net improves Average Precision by 9.7\% over a kinematics-only baseline and achieves a 21.2\% reduction in false-positive ``phantom braking'' events, while a rule-based TTC baseline fails catastrophically (AP = 0.238). Critically, the ARF model produces structured, telemetry-style reasoning traces that make every collision warning auditable.
