  28: \begin{abstract}
  29: Protecting pedestrians and cyclists is one of the hardest parts of urban driving. Simple safety measures such as Time-to-Collision (TTC) mostly depend on distance and relative speed, so they can miss the difference between a pedestrian waiting on the sidewalk and one stepping into the lane. In this paper, we study a direction-aware risk model called the Anisotropic Risk Field (ARF) and pair it with short Evidence-Grounded Reasoning Traces (EGRT) that explain why an alert was raised. The ARF represents the risk around a vulnerable road user as an oriented 2D Gaussian and uses Hessian eigenvalues to describe how the shape of that risk field changes. We first test the idea in a 2D simulator with 5,000 crossing scenarios, where both the baseline and ARF model reach perfect Average Precision (AP), so the main value of the simulator is explanation rather than accuracy. We then test the method on a pseudo-dynamic KITTI protocol built from real scene geometry, sampled VRU velocity priors, and injected positional noise over 7,481 frames and 5,897 VRU detections. On this setup, ARF-Net improves AP from 0.650 to 0.747 over a kinematics-only baseline and reduces false-positive ``phantom braking'' events from 118 to 93, while a TTC baseline reaches only 0.238 AP. A fairness check with a geometry-aware baseline also shows that the proxy KITTI label is strongly tied to explicit scene geometry. For that reason, we treat the KITTI result as evidence that ARF helps over a kinematics-only baseline and provides readable reasoning traces, not as proof that it is the strongest possible model for this proxy task.
  30: \end{abstract}
  31: 
  32: \begin{IEEEkeywords}
  33: Autonomous Vehicles, Vulnerable Road Users, Computer Vision, Simulation, Explainable AI, Anisotropic Risk Fields.
  34: \end{IEEEkeywords}
  35: 
  36: \section{Introduction}
  37: \label{sec:intro}
  38: Autonomous Driving Systems (ADS) are often discussed as a way to reduce crashes caused by human error. Even so, dense urban roads remain difficult because vulnerable road users (VRUs) such as pedestrians, cyclists, and other micromobility users do not move as predictably as lane-following vehicles. They can change speed, direction, and lane position quickly. Because of this, near-miss events involving VRUs remain an important safety problem.
  39: 
  40: Traditional Surrogate Safety Measures (SSMs) are still widely used to estimate collision risk. The most common one is Time-to-Collision (TTC), which measures how long it would take two road users to collide if they kept moving the same way. TTC is simple and fast, but it treats risk as if it spreads equally in all directions. In practice, that is often not enough. A pedestrian waiting near the curb and a pedestrian stepping into the lane can have similar radial distance, yet the second case is clearly more dangerous. This can lead to false alerts, often described as ``phantom braking.''
  41: 
  42: Data-driven models can learn more complex patterns than TTC, but they often produce outputs that are hard to explain. For a safety system, that is a practical problem. If a model raises an alert or recommends braking, an engineer should be able to inspect the reason in simple geometric terms instead of only seeing a confidence score.
  43: 
  44: To bridge this gap, this paper studies an Anisotropic Risk Field (ARF) together with Evidence-Grounded Reasoning Traces (EGRT). The main idea is simple: instead of treating a VRU as a point with one circular danger zone, we model the risk around that VRU as an oriented ellipse. The ellipse stretches differently for pedestrians and cyclists, and its shape changes with motion. From that field, we extract a few geometric features and convert them into short explanation strings.
  45: 
  46: The main contributions of this paper are:
  47: \begin{enumerate}
  48:     \item We formulate an Anisotropic Risk Field (ARF) that uses Hessian eigenvalues of a 2D Gaussian risk field to capture direction-aware changes in risk.
  49:     \item We introduce Evidence-Grounded Reasoning Traces (EGRT), a simple rule-based format that converts model features into short, readable explanations.
  50:     \item We build a 2D kinematic simulator with 5,000 crossing scenarios to test whether the method behaves in a clear and interpretable way.
  51:     \item We evaluate the method on a pseudo-dynamic KITTI protocol and show that ARF improves over a kinematics-only baseline, while also discussing the limits of this proxy setup.
  52: \end{enumerate}
  53: 
  54: \section{Related Work}
  55: \label{sec:related}
  56: 
  57: \subsection{Surrogate Safety Measures}
  58: The foundation of collision avoidance in intelligent transportation relies heavily on Surrogate Safety Measures. Hayward (1972) first formalized Time-to-Collision (TTC), which has since become the gold standard for defining near-miss events \cite{hayward}. Later adaptations include Post-Encroachment Time (PET) and Time-To-React (TTR). However, researchers have repeatedly noted that TTC fails in complex urban intersections where paths are not strictly linear. According to \cite{vogel}, TTC is poorly equipped to handle the lateral movements characteristic of pedestrians, highlighting the need for two-dimensional spatial metrics.
  59: 
  60: \subsection{Artificial Potential Fields}
  61: The concept of Artificial Potential Fields (APF) was introduced by Khatib in the context of robotics to enable real-time obstacle avoidance \cite{khatib}. By assigning repulsive forces to obstacles and attractive forces to the goal, a robot can navigate smoothly. Wang et al. extended this to the ``Driving Safety Field Theory,'' modeling the risk of surrounding traffic \cite{wang}. More recently, Zhang et al. proposed ``Anisotropic Safety Potential Fields,'' recognizing that vehicles and bicycles have directional momentum \cite{zhang}. Our work extends this lineage by specifically tuning the anisotropic covariance matrices to the unique kinematic profiles of distinct VRU classes (pedestrians vs. cyclists) and extracting the Hessian matrix for rapid safety thresholding.
  62: 
  63: \subsection{Explainable AI (XAI) in Autonomous Vehicles}
  64: As regulatory scrutiny over autonomous vehicles increases, explainability has become more important. Methods such as SHAP and Grad-CAM are commonly used to inspect neural networks \cite{lundberg}, but they are often designed for post-hoc analysis and usually produce visual rather than sentence-level explanations. In this work, EGRT is used as a lightweight alternative. It is a rule-based explanation format that turns a small set of geometric features into short semantic traces.
  65: 
  66: \section{Methodology}
  67: \label{sec:method}
  68: 
  69: \subsection{System Architecture}
  70: Our framework consists of three primary modules: the Kinematic Simulator, the ARF Feature Extractor, and the EGRT Generator. The simulator produces state-space representations of AV-VRU interactions. The ARF Extractor processes these coordinates to compute the Hessian matrix of the risk field. Finally, a Gradient Boosting Classifier predicts near-miss events, and the EGRT Generator translates the underlying geometric features into human-readable telemetry logs.
  71: 
  72: \subsection{2D Kinematic Simulation Engine}
  73: Generating a statistically significant dataset of dangerous near-misses in the real world is both difficult and ethically precarious. While large-scale datasets like nuScenes and Waymo Open Dataset exist, extracting thousands of perfectly annotated near-misses is computationally prohibitive. Thus, we engineered a custom 2D kinematic simulator in Python.
  74: 
  75: We define the state of the Autonomous Vehicle (AV) as $S_{AV} = [x_a, y_a, v_a, \theta_a]$, where $(x_a, y_a)$ is the 2D Cartesian position, $v_a$ is the scalar velocity, and $\theta_a$ is the heading angle. Similarly, the VRU state is defined as $S_{VRU} = [x_v, y_v, v_v, \theta_v]$.
  76: 
  77: The simulation operates with a discrete time step $\Delta t = 0.1$ seconds. The position updates follow standard kinematic equations of motion:
  78: \begin{align}
  79:     x(t+1) &= x(t) + v \cdot \cos(\theta) \cdot \Delta t + \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_{noise}) \\
  80:     y(t+1) &= y(t) + v \cdot \sin(\theta) \cdot \Delta t + \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_{noise})
  81: \end{align}
  82: To simulate the unpredictable nature of human walking, we inject Gaussian noise $\mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_{noise})$ into the VRU trajectory, representing slight lateral drifts and micro-adjustments during crossing. The simulator forces paths to intersect, recording the precise frame of minimum Euclidean distance between the entities.
  83: 
  84: \subsection{Anisotropic Risk Field Formulation}
  85: We model the danger zone surrounding a VRU as a dynamically stretched Gaussian distribution. Let $\mathbf{p} = [x_v - x_a, y_v - y_a]^T$ be the relative position vector of the VRU with respect to the AV.
  86: 
  87: We define a diagonal variance matrix $\bm{\Lambda}$ representing the base uncertainty. For pedestrians, lateral uncertainty $\sigma_{lat}$ is heavily weighted, as they can step off a curb instantaneously. For cyclists, longitudinal uncertainty $\sigma_{long}$ dominates due to their forward momentum. Both are modeled as velocity-dependent functions. The specific scalar multipliers (e.g., $\sigma_{lat} = 2.0 + 0.5 v_v$ for pedestrians) were empirically tuned based on average human walking kinematics (1.2--2.0 m/s) and cycling kinematics (4.0--8.0 m/s) from established biomechanics literature:
  88: \begin{equation}
  89:     \bm{\Lambda} = 
  90:     \begin{bmatrix}
  91:     \sigma_{long}^2(v_v) & 0 \\
  92:     0 & \sigma_{lat}^2(v_v)
  93:     \end{bmatrix}
  94: \end{equation}
  95: 
  96: To align the risk field with the VRU's direction of travel, we apply a 2D rotation matrix $\mathbf{R}(\theta_v)$:
  97: \begin{equation}
  98:     \mathbf{R}(\theta_v) = 
  99:     \begin{bmatrix}
 100:     \cos(\theta_v) & -\sin(\theta_v) \\
 101:     \sin(\theta_v) & \cos(\theta_v)
 102:     \end{bmatrix}
 103: \end{equation}
 104: 
 105: The final Anisotropic Covariance Matrix $\bm{\Sigma}$ is computed as:
 106: \begin{equation}
 107:     \bm{\Sigma} = \mathbf{R} \bm{\Lambda} \mathbf{R}^T
 108: \end{equation}
 109: 
 110: The risk potential value $U(\mathbf{p})$ experienced by the AV at its relative distance $\mathbf{p}$ is given by the multivariate Gaussian density function (ignoring the normalization constant for purely relative scaling):
 111: \begin{equation}
 112:     U(\mathbf{p}) = \exp \left( -\frac{1}{2} \mathbf{p}^T \bm{\Sigma}^{-1} \mathbf{p} \right)
 113: \end{equation}
 114: 
 115: \subsection{Hessian Analysis and Risk-Shape Change}
 116: While the risk potential $U(\mathbf{p})$ provides a scalar value of danger, it lacks geometric context. To understand \textit{how} the risk field is changing---i.e., whether the AV is piercing the side of the field (lateral crossing) or approaching it head-on---we compute the Hessian matrix $\mathbf{H}$ of the risk potential:
 117: \begin{equation}
 118:     \mathbf{H} = U(\mathbf{p}) \cdot \left( (\bm{\Sigma}^{-1}\mathbf{p})(\bm{\Sigma}^{-1}\mathbf{p})^T - \bm{\Sigma}^{-1} \right)
 119: \end{equation}
 120: 
 121: We perform an eigendecomposition on $\mathbf{H}$ to extract the principal curvatures, denoted as eigenvalues $\lambda_1$ and $\lambda_2$. By sorting these such that $\lambda_2$ corresponds to the longitudinal axis and $\lambda_1$ corresponds to the lateral axis, we can interpret the geometric nature of the threat. Large changes in these eigenvalues indicate that the shape of the risk field is changing quickly.
 122: If $\lambda_2 \gg \lambda_1$, the danger is primarily longitudinal (e.g., a cyclist braking suddenly). If $\lambda_1 \gg \lambda_2$, the danger is lateral (e.g., a pedestrian stepping out from between parked cars).
 123: 
 124: \subsection{Evidence-Grounded Reasoning Traces (EGRT)}
 125: To improve interpretability, we designed an EGRT generator that maps the computed eigenvalues ($\lambda_1, \lambda_2$) and the Euclidean distance to short, human-readable strings. Instead of returning only a binary decision, the system also logs a compact explanation in the following format:
 126: 
 127: \begin{quote}
 128: \texttt{[EGRT] TRG:RUPTURE | L2:0.10 > L1:0.06 | DIST:1.2m | DYN:LONGITUDINAL | ACTION:BRAKE}
 129: \end{quote}
 130: 
 131: This structure allows a reviewer to inspect why an alert was raised and whether the dominant motion pattern was longitudinal, lateral, or mixed.
 132: 
 133: \section{Experimental Evaluation}
 134: \label{sec:eval}
 135: 
 136: \subsection{Simulation Dataset Generation}
 137: Using our Python-based kinematic simulator, we generated an extensive dataset of 5,000 distinct interactions. Each interaction randomly assigned the VRU as either a pedestrian or a cyclist, initialized random starting velocities (18--54 km/h for the AV), and computed crossing trajectories. We specifically injected 20\% of the data as ``edge cases'' featuring significant trajectory noise, simulating momentary sensor occlusion or tracking jitter. A ground truth ``near-miss'' label was strictly defined by a minimum physical distance of less than 3.0 meters.
 138: 
 139: \subsection{Model Training}
 140: We partitioned the dataset using a 70-30 train-test split. We trained two models for comparative analysis:
 141: \begin{itemize}
 142:     \item \textbf{Isotropic Baseline Model}: A Gradient Boosting Classifier (100 estimators, max depth 4) trained solely on raw kinematics: Euclidean distance, AV velocity, VRU velocity, and relative velocity.
 143:     \item \textbf{Proposed ARF-Net}: An identical Gradient Boosting Classifier trained on the baseline features, augmented with our proposed Anisotropic Risk Field features: the Hessian eigenvalues ($\lambda_1, \lambda_2$) and the determinant of the Hessian.
 144: \end{itemize}
 145: 
 146: \subsection{Simulation Results and Discussion}
 147: 
 148: The quantitative performance of the models is detailed in Table \ref{tab:results}. 
 149: 
 150: \begin{table}[htbp]
 151: \centering
 152: \caption{Average Precision (AP) on Simulated Kinematic Data}
 153: \label{tab:results}
 154: \begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
 155: \toprule
 156: \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Overall AP} & \textbf{Pedestrian AP} & \textbf{Cyclist AP} \\ \midrule
 157: Baseline & 1.000 & 1.000 & 1.000 \\
 158: ARF-Net & 1.000 & 1.000 & 1.000 \\ \bottomrule
 159: \end{tabular}
 160: \end{table}
 161: 
 162: As illustrated, both the Isotropic Baseline and the proposed ARF-Net achieved a perfect Average Precision (AP) of 1.0 on the test set. These results reflect the simplicity of the simulator. In a clean 2D setting without real perception errors, a Gradient Boosting Classifier can learn the strict 3.0-meter ground truth threshold from the distance feature alone.
 163: 
 164: \textbf{Explainability as the Core Contribution:} 
 165: The purpose of the simulation experiment is not to show an accuracy gain---that is not possible when both models already reach perfect AP---but to establish \textit{clear, rule-based explainability}. 
 166: 
 167: When the Isotropic Baseline model successfully predicts a near-miss, it acts as an opaque ensemble of decision trees. If deployed on an actual vehicle, an engineer probing a false positive would only see a set of split conditions across hundreds of trees. 
 168: 
 169: Conversely, the ARF-Net successfully categorizes the danger while simultaneously outputting the underlying physics reasoning. Consider the following samples directly extracted from our test suite:
 170: 
 171: \begin{itemize}
 172:     \item \texttt{Sample 1 (True Positive):} \\ \texttt{[EGRT] TRG:RUPTURE | L1:0.1432 > L2:0.0450 | DIST:2.1m | DYN:LATERAL\_AGILITY | ACTION:EVADE}
 173:     \item \texttt{Sample 2 (True Negative):} \\ \texttt{[EGRT] STAT:SAFE | ESCAPE\_MANIFOLD:INTACT | THREAT:NONE}
 174: \end{itemize}
 175: 
 176: Sample 1 suggests that the model is capturing a lateral entry case in a way that can be explained with simple geometric terms. That kind of transparency is useful for safety review and debugging.
 177: 
 178: \section{Pseudo-Dynamic Evaluation on Real-World KITTI Geometry}
 179: \label{sec:kitti}
 180: 
 181: \subsection{Dataset and Ground Truth}
 182: To evaluate the ARF-Net beyond pristine simulation, we transitioned the framework to the KITTI Vision Benchmark \cite{geiger}. We processed all 7,481 training frames, extracting 5,897 VRU detections (Pedestrian and Cyclist classes). To isolate the performance of the risk-assessment module---rather than conflating it with the failure modes of a specific object detector---we parse KITTI's ground-truth \texttt{label\_2} annotations and intentionally inject synthetic Gaussian noise ($\sigma = 0.3$m, applied to 20\% of samples) to simulate realistic bounding box jitter. Because velocities are synthetically sampled, this section constitutes a pseudo-dynamic evaluation on real-world spatial geometry rather than a fully dynamic behavioral validation.
 183: 
 184: Since KITTI provides single frames rather than tracked sequences, true per-frame velocities are unavailable. We therefore estimate VRU velocities by sampling from class-typical kinematic priors: $v \sim U(0.5, 2.0)$ m/s for pedestrians and $v \sim U(2.0, 8.0)$ m/s for cyclists, oriented along the annotated \texttt{rotation\_y} heading. To mitigate data leakage, we employ a frame-ID-based 70-30 train-test split rather than randomly splitting bounding boxes. We acknowledge that since KITTI frames are sampled from driving routes, some sequential inter-frame correlation may persist.
 185: 
 186: We define a composite ground truth label based on physical risk geometry rather than a single distance threshold. A VRU is labeled as ``high-risk'' if it satisfies three simultaneous conditions: (1) Euclidean distance $< 15$m from the AV, (2) lateral offset $|x| < 3$m from the AV's forward axis (i.e., the VRU is in or near the driving lane), and (3) the VRU is in front of the AV ($z > 0$). This creates a geometry-based proxy label. In other words, the KITTI experiment evaluates how well different feature sets recover this proxy label, not a fully detector-independent measure of real-world collision risk.
 187: 
 188: \subsection{Baseline Comparisons}
 189: We evaluate three main approaches:
 190: \begin{itemize}
 191:     \item \textbf{Rule-Based TTC ($< 3.0$s)}: The standard Time-to-Collision metric, computed using the radial closing speed between the AV and VRU.
 192:     \item \textbf{ML Baseline}: A Gradient Boosting Classifier trained on raw kinematics (distance, AV velocity, VRU velocity, relative velocity).
 193:     \item \textbf{Proposed ARF-Net}: The same classifier augmented with the Hessian eigenvalues ($\lambda_1, \lambda_2$) and determinant.
 194: \end{itemize}
 195: 
 196: \subsection{Results}
 197: 
 198: \begin{table}[htbp]
 199: \centering
 200: \caption{Performance on the KITTI Vision Benchmark}
 201: \label{tab:kitti_results}
 202: \begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
 203: \toprule
 204: \textbf{Metric} & \textbf{TTC ($<3.0$s)} & \textbf{ML Baseline} & \textbf{ARF-Net} \\ \midrule
 205: Average Precision (AP) & 0.238 & 0.650 & \textbf{0.747} \\
 206: False Positives & 370 & 118 & \textbf{93} \\
 207: False Negatives & --- & 118 & \textbf{96} \\ \bottomrule
 208: \end{tabular}
 209: \end{table}
 210: 
 211: The rule-based TTC threshold performed poorly on this proxy task (AP = 0.238, 370 false positives). This is consistent with the fact that the label depends on lateral position and forward position in addition to distance. The ML Baseline improved to AP = 0.650, but still generated 118 false-positive alerts.
 212: 
 213: The ARF-Net reached AP = 0.747, which is a \textbf{9.7\% absolute improvement} over the kinematics-only baseline. It also reduced false positives from 118 to 93---a \textbf{21.2\% reduction in phantom braking}---and reduced false negatives from 118 to 96.
 214: 
 215: To check whether this gain comes from the ARF formulation itself or from the baseline design, we also trained a geometry-aware baseline using only $[\text{distance}, |x|, z]$ on the same split. That model reached AP = 1.000. This shows that the current KITTI proxy label is strongly tied to explicit scene geometry and can be recovered almost perfectly when those variables are provided directly. For that reason, we interpret the KITTI result conservatively: ARF improves over a kinematics-only baseline and provides readable reasoning traces, but this experiment does not prove that ARF is the strongest possible model for this proxy task.
 216: 
 217: \subsection{Limitations}
 218: \label{sec:limitations}
 219: We acknowledge several limitations of the current evaluation. First, the KITTI proxy label is explicitly defined from geometric variables. As the geometry-aware baseline shows, this makes the task easier when those same variables are given directly to the classifier. Second, VRU velocities on KITTI were estimated from class-typical kinematic priors rather than measured from tracked sequences; future work should test the framework on multi-frame datasets where motion can be estimated directly. Third, the injected Gaussian noise is only a rough approximation of real perception error; real sensor failures and occlusion patterns are more complex. Finally, while the Gradient Boosting Classifier is easy to inspect and sufficient for this study, the main goal here is interpretability and careful comparison rather than maximizing model complexity.
 220: 
 221: \section{Conclusion and Future Work}
 222: \label{sec:conclusion}
 223: This paper studied an explainable direction-aware safety framework for autonomous driving scenarios involving vulnerable road users. By moving beyond isotropic measures such as Time-to-Collision, we modeled pedestrian and cyclist risk with an Anisotropic Risk Field and translated the resulting geometric features into short Evidence-Grounded Reasoning Traces (EGRT).
 224: 
 225: On the pseudo-dynamic KITTI protocol used in this study, ARF features improved performance over a kinematics-only baseline, giving a 9.7\% AP gain and a 21.2\% reduction in false-positive alerts. At the same time, a geometry-aware baseline showed that the proxy label can be solved very well from explicit scene geometry alone. For that reason, the safest conclusion is that ARF is useful as an interpretable feature set and explanation mechanism in this setup, rather than proof of overall superiority on real-world near-miss prediction.

