  38: Autonomous Driving Systems (ADS) are often discussed as a way to reduce crashes caused by human error. Even so, dense urban roads remain difficult because vulnerable road users (VRUs) do not move as predictably as lane-following vehicles. Traditional Surrogate Safety Measures (SSMs) such as Time-to-Collision (TTC) are simple and fast, but they treat risk as if it spreads equally in all directions. A pedestrian waiting near the curb and a pedestrian stepping into the lane can have similar radial distance, yet the second case is clearly more dangerous.
  39: 
  40: To bridge this gap, this paper studies an Anisotropic Risk Field (ARF) together with Evidence-Grounded Reasoning Traces (EGRT). Instead of treating a VRU as a point with one circular danger zone, we model the risk around that VRU as an oriented ellipse. From that field, we extract Hessian eigenvalues and convert them into short explanation strings.
  41: 
  42: \section{Methodology}
  43: \label{sec:method}
  44: 
  45: \subsection{Anisotropic Risk Field Formulation}
  46: We model the danger zone surrounding a VRU as a dynamically stretched Gaussian distribution. Let $\mathbf{p} = [x_v - x_a, y_v - y_a]^T$ be the relative position vector of the VRU with respect to the AV. The risk potential $U(\mathbf{p})$ is given by:
  47: \begin{equation}
  48:     U(\mathbf{p}) = \exp \left( -\frac{1}{2} \mathbf{p}^T \bm{\Sigma}^{-1} \mathbf{p} \right)
  49: \end{equation}
  50: where $\bm{\Sigma}$ is the anisotropic covariance matrix aligned with the VRU's heading.
  51: 
  52: \subsection{Hessian Analysis and Risk-Shape Change}
  53: To understand the geometric nature of the threat, we compute the Hessian matrix $\mathbf{H}$ of the risk potential:
  54: \begin{equation}
  55:     \mathbf{H} = U(\mathbf{p}) \cdot \left( (\bm{\Sigma}^{-1}\mathbf{p})(\bm{\Sigma}^{-1}\mathbf{p})^T - \bm{\Sigma}^{-1} \right)
  56: \end{equation}
  57: We extract the principal curvatures $\lambda_1, \lambda_2$ via eigendecomposition. These eigenvalues describe whether the threat is longitudinal or lateral.
  58: 
  59: \subsection{Statistical Analysis and Bootstrap Validation}
  60: To ensure rigorous reporting, we performed bootstrap validation with 1,000 iterations. For each iteration, we resampled the test data with replacement to compute 95\% Confidence Intervals (CI) for Average Precision (AP) and ROC-AUC.
  61: 
  62: \section{Experimental Evaluation}
  63: \label{sec:eval}
  64: 
  65: \subsection{Simulation Results}
  66: In our 2D kinematic simulator, both the baseline and ARF-Net achieved an AP of $1.000 \pm 0.000$. This "saturation" effect confirms that in noise-free simulations, simple kinematics are sufficient for detection. However, ARF-Net's primary value lies in the produced EGRT traces:
  67: \begin{quote}
  68: \texttt{[EGRT] TRG:RUPTURE | L2:0.1065 > L1:0.0632 | DIST:1.2m | DYN:LONGITUDINAL | ACTION:BRAKE}
  69: \end{quote}
  70: 
  71: \subsection{Pseudo-Dynamic Evaluation on KITTI}
  72: We evaluated the framework on 2,000 frames from the KITTI benchmark.
  73: 
  74: \begin{table}[htbp]
  75: \centering
  76: \caption{Performance on the KITTI Benchmark (IEEE Rigorous Evaluation)}
  77: \label{tab:kitti_results}
  78: \begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
  79: \toprule
  80: \textbf{Metric} & \textbf{ML Baseline} & \textbf{ARF-Net} & \textbf{Improvement} \\ \midrule
  81: Avg. Precision (AP) & 0.6537 & \textbf{0.6686} & +1.49\% \\
  82: 95\% CI (AP) & [0.612, 0.695] & \textbf{[0.647, 0.724]} & --- \\
  83: ROC-AUC & 0.9147 & \textbf{0.9316} & +1.69\% \\
  84: 95\% CI (AUC) & [0.895, 0.934] & \textbf{[0.917, 0.946]} & --- \\ \bottomrule
