 137: 
 138: The paired bootstrap result is the strongest quantitative evidence in this study. It shows that ARF consistently improves over the kinematics-only baseline on this split. At the same time, the geometry audit result prevents overclaiming. Because the proxy label is defined from distance, lateral offset, and forward position, a model given those values directly can nearly reconstruct the label.
 139: 
 140: \section{Limitations}
 141: This study has four main limitations. First, the KITTI task is pseudo-dynamic because VRU velocities are sampled rather than measured from tracks. Second, the high-risk label is a proxy label built from geometry, not a manually annotated near-miss event. Third, the geometry audit baseline shows that the proxy task can be solved almost directly from the same variables used to define it. Fourth, the EGRT trace is an explanation of the model evidence, not proof that the system would behave safely in a real vehicle.
 142: 
 143: These limitations do not make the study useless, but they narrow the claim. The fair claim is that ARF gives a compact, explainable feature layer that improves over simple kinematics on a geometry-based proxy task. A stronger future study should use real tracked video, manually reviewed near-miss events, and a sequence-level split.
 144: 
 145: \section{Conclusion}
 146: This project studied an interpretable, direction-aware risk model for vulnerable road user near-miss detection. The main result is modest but defensible: ARF improves over a kinematics-only baseline on a KITTI pseudo-dynamic proxy task, and paired bootstrap validation shows that the improvement is usually positive. The geometry audit baseline also shows an important caution: when a label is built from explicit scene geometry, geometry-aware models can solve it very well. For an undergraduate research project, the useful contribution is the combination of ARF features, readable EGRT traces, honest baselines, and reproducible evaluation.
